              "The PMI data was only the latest indication that the Chinese economy is slowing while the U.S. economy is booming. Chinese GDP slowed to 6.7 percent in the second quarter compared with the first quarter and some economists believe it could go as low as 5 percent once the full effects of a trade slowdown get factored in.",
              "The Chinese currency, called the renminbi, has plummeted 8 percent in recent months, a huge move for a major currency. The currency is allowed to float within a band by the Chinese central bank, but the government is clearly happy to let it decline as it helps offset the impact of President Trump’s tariffs of $34 billion worth of Chinese goods",
              "U.S. GDP growth hit an annual 4.1 percent in the second quarter, the fastest growth in four years. The S&P 500 stock index is up almost 14 percent in the last year, while the U.S. Dollar is booming against other major currencies.\n\nOne indication that China is feeling the pain more than the U.S. Is that the Chinese government called for new talks with Washington to settle their trade differences"
